      "notes": "\n<h2>Message and transmission medium</h2>\n\n<p>In data communication, the <b>message</b> is the information being communicated, while the <b>transmission medium</b> is the path or material through which the information travels.</p>\n\n<h3>1. The message</h3>\n<p>A message is the data sent from a sender to a receiver. It can take different forms, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Text, such as an email or text message.</li>\n<li>Numbers, such as examination marks.</li>\n<li>Images, such as a photograph.</li>\n<li>Audio, such as a recorded voice message.</li>\n<li>Video, such as a recorded lesson.</li>\n<li>Files, such as a word-processing document.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The transmission medium</h3>\n<p>A transmission medium carries signals from one device to another. It may be wired or wireless.</p>\n\n<h3>Wired media</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Twisted-pair cable:</b> Uses pairs of insulated copper wires twisted together. It is commonly used in Ethernet networks.</li>\n<li><b>Coaxial cable:</b> Uses a central conductor surrounded by insulation and shielding.</li>\n<li><b>Fibre-optic cable:</b> Carries information as pulses of light through optical fibres.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Wireless media</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Radio waves:</b> Used by technologies such as Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.</li>\n<li><b>Microwaves:</b> Used in certain point-to-point and telecommunications links.</li>\n<li><b>Infrared:</b> Used for short-range communication in some devices.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. How the message travels</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>The sender creates a message.</li>\n<li>The device represents the message as data and prepares signals for transmission.</li>\n<li>The signals travel through a suitable medium.</li>\n<li>The receiving device detects and interprets the signals to recover the data.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>A learner sends a photograph to a friend using Wi-Fi.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Message:</b> The photograph's digital data.</li>\n<li><b>Transmission medium:</b> The wireless radio link between the device and the Wi-Fi access point, followed by the relevant network links.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The photograph is the information being transferred. Wi-Fi radio signals carry data over the wireless part of the connection.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Calling a cable the message rather than the medium.</li>\n<li>Assuming that the medium and the message are the same thing.</li>\n<li>Assuming that all wireless communication uses the same type of signal or operates over the same distance.</li>\n</ul>\n",
