      "notes": "\n<h2>Full-duplex communication</h2>\n\n<p><b>Full-duplex communication</b> allows data to travel in both directions simultaneously. Each endpoint can transmit and receive at the same time through a full-duplex communication arrangement.</p>\n\n<h3>How it works</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Device A sends data to Device B.</li>\n<li>At the same time, Device B can send data to Device A.</li>\n<li>Both devices can receive incoming data while transmitting outgoing data.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Telephone conversation:</b> Both people can speak and hear one another at the same time.</li>\n<li><b>Video call:</b> Participants can send audio and video while receiving the other participants' audio and video.</li>\n<li><b>Full-duplex Ethernet link:</b> Compatible devices can transmit and receive simultaneously over a suitable network link.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Advantages</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Allows simultaneous two-way communication.</li>\n<li>Reduces the need to take turns transmitting.</li>\n<li>Supports interactive activities such as calls and video conferencing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Limitations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>It may require suitable hardware and communication arrangements.</li>\n<li>It can involve greater implementation complexity than a simple one-way system.</li>\n<li>Simultaneous communication does not guarantee high speed; performance still depends on bandwidth, congestion, equipment and other factors.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Compare the three communication modes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Simplex:</b> Data travels in one direction only.</li>\n<li><b>Half-duplex:</b> Data travels in both directions, but the endpoints take turns transmitting.</li>\n<li><b>Full-duplex:</b> Data travels in both directions simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>Two people are having a telephone conversation. Person A speaks while Person B listens. Person B can begin replying before Person A has completely finished speaking.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Person A's voice is transmitted to Person B.</li>\n<li>Person B's voice can simultaneously be transmitted to Person A.</li>\n<li>Both directions operate at the same time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This illustrates full-duplex communication.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confusing full-duplex with half-duplex because both support two-way communication.</li>\n<li>Assuming full-duplex automatically means unlimited speed.</li>\n<li>Assuming every telephone or network technology implements full-duplex in exactly the same way.</li>\n</ul>\n",
