      "notes": "\n<h2>Network interface card</h2>\n\n<p>A <b>network interface card (NIC)</b> is a hardware component that enables a computer or another device to connect to a network. A NIC may provide a wired Ethernet connection or a wireless network connection.</p>\n\n<h3>1. Functions of a NIC</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Provides the hardware interface used to connect a device to a network.</li>\n<li>Transmits and receives network data.</li>\n<li>Handles link-level communication functions according to the network technology.</li>\n<li>Provides a network interface that the device's operating system and networking software can use.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Types of network interface</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Wired NIC:</b> Provides a physical network connection, commonly through an Ethernet port.</li>\n<li><b>Wireless NIC:</b> Enables a device to communicate wirelessly, for example through Wi-Fi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A NIC may be built into the motherboard, installed as an expansion card or connected externally through a suitable interface.</p>\n\n<h3>3. MAC address</h3>\n<p>A network interface commonly has a Media Access Control (MAC) address used for communication on the local network link. The address is associated with a network interface, although software can sometimes change the address presented by a device.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Why a NIC is important</h3>\n<p>A computer needs a suitable network interface to exchange data over the chosen network technology. For example, a computer without a suitable wireless interface cannot connect directly to Wi-Fi unless an appropriate wireless adapter is added.</p>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>A desktop computer has an Ethernet port but no built-in Wi-Fi adapter. The school wants to connect it to a wireless network.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The existing Ethernet interface supports a wired connection.</li>\n<li>The desktop needs a suitable wireless network interface for direct Wi-Fi access.</li>\n<li>The school can install or connect a compatible wireless adapter.</li>\n<li>The adapter's drivers and network settings must also be working correctly.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Confusing a NIC with a router or switch.</li>\n<li>Assuming every NIC is a separate card plugged into the motherboard.</li>\n<li>Assuming a NIC alone provides internet access without a suitable network connection and configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n",
