      "notes": "\n<h2>Hub</h2>\n\n<p>A <b>network hub</b> is a basic networking device that connects multiple devices in a local area network. A traditional Ethernet hub repeats incoming signals out through its other ports rather than forwarding data only to the intended destination port.</p>\n\n<h3>1. How a hub works</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>A connected device sends an Ethernet signal to the hub.</li>\n<li>The hub repeats the incoming signal through its other ports.</li>\n<li>Connected devices receive the signal and determine whether the transmitted frame is intended for them.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>2. Characteristics of a traditional hub</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Operates at the physical layer of the traditional OSI networking model.</li>\n<li>Does not learn MAC addresses to select a destination port.</li>\n<li>Repeats signals to other ports instead of intelligently forwarding frames to one destination port.</li>\n<li>Devices connected through a traditional hub share the collision domain and available bandwidth.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Advantages</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple to use for basic network connections.</li>\n<li>Can connect several devices to a common network segment.</li>\n<li>Requires little configuration in a basic installation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Limitations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Unnecessary traffic is repeated to multiple connected devices.</li>\n<li>Shared bandwidth can reduce network efficiency.</li>\n<li>Traditional hubs use a shared collision domain, which can lead to collisions in half-duplex Ethernet.</li>\n<li>They offer less efficient traffic handling than modern switches.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>Three computers are connected to a traditional Ethernet hub. Computer A sends a frame intended for Computer B.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The hub receives the signal from Computer A.</li>\n<li>The hub repeats the signal through its other ports, including the port connected to Computer C.</li>\n<li>Computer B processes the frame if it is addressed to B.</li>\n<li>Computer C receives the repeated signal but ignores the frame if it is not intended for C.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Hub compared with a switch</h3>\n<p>A traditional hub repeats signals to other ports. A switch learns MAC addresses and forwards Ethernet frames toward the appropriate port when it knows where the destination is located.</p>\n\n<h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Claiming that a traditional hub sends a frame only to the intended destination port.</li>\n<li>Confusing a hub with a router, which forwards packets between networks.</li>\n<li>Assuming hubs and switches handle traffic in the same way.</li>\n</ul>\n",
