      "notes": "\n<h2>Websites and Webpages</h2>\n\nA webpage is an individual web resource that can be accessed through a web browser. A website is a collection of related web resources, commonly organised under a domain name.\n\n<h3>Webpages</h3>\n\nA webpage may contain text, pictures, tables, videos, links and interactive elements. It may be created using technologies such as HTML, CSS and JavaScript.\n\nEach webpage is generally identified by its own URL, although one URL can sometimes produce different content depending on the user or circumstances.\n\nExamples of webpages include:\n- A school's admissions page.\n- A page containing a lesson.\n- A contact page showing an organisation's details.\n\n<h3>Websites</h3>\n\nA website groups related webpages and other resources. A school website might contain a home page, admissions information, subject resources, news and contact details.\n\nThe home page commonly introduces the site and links to important sections, but not every website uses the same structure.\n\n<h3>How they are connected</h3>\n\n1. A website contains related resources.\n2. Each webpage provides particular information or functions.\n3. Hyperlinks help users move between pages.\n4. URLs identify the resources users want to access.\n\n<h3>Example</h3>\n\nImagine a school website called school.example. Its home page links to an admissions page and a timetable page. The complete collection is the website; the admissions page and timetable page are individual webpages.\n\n<h3>Important distinction</h3>\n\nA website is not the same as a web browser. The website contains resources, while the browser is software used to request and display them.\n",
