      "notes": "\n<h2>Running a program</h2>\n<p><strong>Running a program</strong> means executing its instructions in a suitable environment so that the specified operations are carried out.</p>\n\n<h3>Running a Python program</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Write valid Python source code in an editor.</li>\n<li>Save it in a file, for example <code>hello.py</code>.</li>\n<li>Make sure Python is installed or available through a suitable environment.</li>\n<li>Open a terminal or use the editor's run facility.</li>\n<li>Execute the file, for example with <code>python hello.py</code> where that command is configured for Python.</li>\n<li>Observe the output and any error messages.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Example program</h3>\n<pre><code>print(\"Program started\")\nprint(5 + 7)\nprint(\"Program finished\")</code></pre>\n<p>Expected output:</p>\n<pre><code>Program started\n12\nProgram finished</code></pre>\n\n<h3>What can happen when a program runs?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Successful execution:</strong> The program performs its instructions without an unhandled error.</li>\n<li><strong>Syntax or compilation error:</strong> The language tools reject invalid code before the relevant execution can proceed.</li>\n<li><strong>Runtime error:</strong> The program encounters a problem during execution, such as an attempt to divide an integer by zero.</li>\n<li><strong>Logic error:</strong> The program runs but produces a result that does not meet the requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Running is not the same as testing</h3>\n<p>Running executes the program. Testing checks whether its behaviour is correct for selected inputs and conditions. A program can run successfully and still have a logic error.</p>\n\n<h3>When a program does not run</h3>\n<p>Check that the file is saved, the correct file is being executed, the required runtime is available and the error message is understood. Correct the underlying issue and run the program again.</p>\n",
