      "notes": "\n<h3>Meaning of Boolean Values</h3>\nA Boolean value represents one of two logical states: <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>. Boolean values are commonly used to make decisions in programs.\n\n<pre><code>is_registered = True\nhas_paid_fees = False\n\nprint(is_registered)\nprint(has_paid_fees)</code></pre>\n\nIn Python, the Boolean literals must begin with capital letters: <code>True</code> and <code>False</code>.\n\n<h3>Boolean Expressions</h3>\nA comparison can produce a Boolean result.\n\n<pre><code>age = 18\n\nprint(age >= 18)  # True\nprint(age < 18)   # False</code></pre>\n\nThe first expression asks whether age is at least 18. The second asks whether it is below 18.\n\n<h3>Using Booleans in Conditions</h3>\n<pre><code>has_ticket = True\n\nif has_ticket:\n    print(\"You may enter.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"A ticket is required.\")</code></pre>\n\nThe program follows the first branch because <code>has_ticket</code> is <code>True</code>.\n\n<h3>Boolean Results Are Not the Same as Text</h3>\n<pre><code>answer = True\nmessage = \"True\"\n\nprint(type(answer))   # &lt;class 'bool'&gt;\nprint(type(message))  # &lt;class 'str'&gt;</code></pre>\n\nThe value <code>True</code> is Boolean, while <code>\"True\"</code> is a string containing text.\n",
