      "notes": "\n<h3>Meaning of a Counter-Controlled Loop</h3>\nA counter-controlled loop repeats a known number of times. A counter or sequence of values determines how many repetitions occur.\n\n<h3>Using range()</h3>\nPython's <code>range()</code> is commonly used with a for loop.\n\n<pre><code>for count in range(1, 6):\n    print(count)</code></pre>\n\nThis prints the integers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The start value is included, but the stop value 6 is excluded.\n\n<h3>Specifying a Step</h3>\n<pre><code>for number in range(2, 11, 2):\n    print(number)</code></pre>\n\nOutput:\n<pre><code>2\n4\n6\n8\n10</code></pre>\n\nThe third argument specifies the step size. Here, the value increases by 2 each time.\n\n<h3>Counting with a While Loop</h3>\n<pre><code>count = 1\n\nwhile count <= 4:\n    print(count)\n    count += 1</code></pre>\n\nThe counter is updated in every repetition. Without that update, the loop may not terminate.\n\n<h3>Counting Repetitions</h3>\nFor <code>range(start, stop)</code> with a positive step of 1, the generated values begin at start and end at stop minus 1. For example, <code>range(3, 7)</code> generates 3, 4, 5, and 6: four values.\n\n<h3>Common Off-by-One Error</h3>\nIf a task needs to print numbers from 1 through 5, <code>range(1, 5)</code> is not enough because 5 is excluded. Use <code>range(1, 6)</code>.\n",
