      "notes": "\n<h2>Updating elements in a list</h2>\n<p>Updating an element means changing the value stored at a particular position in a list. In Python, use the element's index on the left side of an assignment statement.</p>\n<pre><code>marks = [45, 60, 72]\nmarks[1] = 80\n\nprint(marks)</code></pre>\n<p>Output: <code>[45, 80, 72]</code></p>\n<p>Python list indexes begin at 0. Therefore, index 1 refers to the second element. Updating an element changes the existing value rather than adding a new element.</p>\n<p><strong>Common error:</strong> using an index that does not exist causes an IndexError.</p>\n<pre><code>marks = [45, 60, 72]\nmarks[3] = 90  # IndexError</code></pre>\n",
