      "notes": "\n<h2>Purpose of functions</h2>\n<p>A function is a named block of code that performs a particular task. A function can be called whenever its task is needed.</p>\n<p>Functions help programmers reuse code, divide a large problem into smaller parts and make programs easier to test and maintain.</p>\n<pre><code>def greet():\n    print(\"Welcome to Tixar\")\n\ngreet()\ngreet()</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code>Welcome to Tixar\nWelcome to Tixar</code></pre>\n<p>The function is defined once but called twice. Defining a function does not normally execute its body; the function runs when it is called.</p>\n<p>A function can accept inputs through parameters and can return a result to the code that called it.</p>\n",
