      "notes": "\n<h2>Reusing code</h2>\n<p>Code reuse means using an existing function or code block again instead of rewriting the same instructions. Functions are one of the main ways to reuse code.</p>\n<pre><code>def calculate_total(price, quantity):\n    return price * quantity\n\nfirst_total = calculate_total(100, 2)\nsecond_total = calculate_total(50, 3)\n\nprint(first_total)\nprint(second_total)</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code>200\n150</code></pre>\n<p>The same function handles different inputs. If the calculation needs correction, the programmer can update the function once rather than changing multiple copies of the calculation.</p>\n<p>Reusable code should have a clear purpose, suitable inputs and predictable results. Reuse does not mean copying code without checking whether it fits the new task.</p>\n",
