      "notes": "\n<h2>Syntax errors</h2>\n<p>A syntax error occurs when code breaks the rules of the programming language. Python may be unable to parse the code and will report an error before the affected code can run normally.</p>\n<p><strong>Incorrect code:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>age = 16\nif age >= 18\n    print(\"Adult\")</code></pre>\n<p>The colon is missing after the condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Corrected code:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>age = 16\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\"Adult\")</code></pre>\n<p>Other common causes include unmatched parentheses, missing quotation marks and incorrect indentation. Read the error message and inspect the reported line and nearby lines.</p>\n<p>Fixing a syntax error does not guarantee the program's logic is correct; the program must still be tested.</p>\n",
