      "notes": "\n<h2>Uses of databases</h2>\n<p>Databases are used wherever organisations need to store, organise, retrieve and update related information.</p>\n<h3>Common uses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Education:</strong> student details, attendance, marks and enrolment.</li>\n<li><strong>Banking:</strong> customer accounts, transactions and balances.</li>\n<li><strong>Healthcare:</strong> patient details, appointments and treatment records.</li>\n<li><strong>Retail:</strong> products, stock levels, sales and customer orders.</li>\n<li><strong>Transport:</strong> bookings, routes and passenger information.</li>\n<li><strong>Government:</strong> registrations, licences and service records.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Example: shop stock</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Code</th><th>Product</th><th>Quantity</th></tr>\n<tr><td>P01</td><td>Exercise book</td><td>120</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P02</td><td>Pen</td><td>85</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>A shop can use this information to check stock, update quantities after sales and identify products that need restocking.</p>\n<p>The type of data stored depends on the organisation's purpose. Access to sensitive records should be limited to authorised users.</p>\n",
