      "notes": "<h2>Sorting records</h2>\n<p>Sorting arranges query results in a chosen order, such as alphabetical order or lowest to highest. In SQL, ORDER BY specifies the field used to sort the results.</p>\n<h3>Ascending order</h3>\n<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age\nFROM Students\nORDER BY FullName ASC;</code></pre>\n<p><code>ASC</code> means ascending order. It is the default sort direction in SQL when no direction is specified.</p>\n<h3>Descending order</h3>\n<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age\nFROM Students\nORDER BY Age DESC;</code></pre>\n<p><code>DESC</code> means descending order. For numeric values, this generally puts larger values before smaller ones.</p>\n<p>Sorting changes the order in which results are presented, not the stored values themselves.</p>",
