      "notes": "<h2>Filtering records</h2>\n<p>Filtering records means selecting only the records that satisfy a specified condition. In SQL, the WHERE clause is commonly used to filter query results.</p>\n<h3>Example</h3>\n<pre><code>SELECT FullName, Age\nFROM Students\nWHERE Age > 16;</code></pre>\n<p>This retrieves the names and ages of students whose ages are greater than 16.</p>\n<h3>Common comparison operators</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>=</code> Equal to</li>\n<li><code>&lt;&gt;</code> Not equal to</li>\n<li><code>&gt;</code> Greater than</li>\n<li><code>&lt;</code> Less than</li>\n<li><code>&gt;=</code> Greater than or equal to</li>\n<li><code>&lt;=</code> Less than or equal to</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Combining conditions</h3>\n<pre><code>SELECT *\nFROM Students\nWHERE Age >= 16 AND ClassID = 2;</code></pre>\n<p>AND requires both conditions to be true. OR requires at least one condition to be true.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> filtering determines which records appear in the result; it does not delete the other records.</p>",
