      "notes": "<h2>INSERT statements</h2>\n<p>INSERT INTO adds new records to a table. A column list makes it clear which fields receive the supplied values.</p>\n<h3>Example</h3>\n<pre><code>INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName, Age)\nVALUES (101, 'Amina Otieno', 16);</code></pre>\n<p>The first value is assigned to StudentID, the second to FullName, and the third to Age.</p>\n<h3>Insert into selected columns</h3>\n<pre><code>INSERT INTO Students (StudentID, FullName)\nVALUES (102, 'Brian Kamau');</code></pre>\n<p>This is valid only if omitted fields can accept NULL or have suitable defaults, and the table's other constraints are satisfied.</p>\n<h3>Common errors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Providing a different number of values from the listed columns.</li>\n<li>Putting values in the wrong order.</li>\n<li>Using a duplicate primary key.</li>\n<li>Violating a required field or data-type constraint.</li>\n</ul>",
