      "notes": "\n<h3>1. Check the output</h3>\n<p>Before printing a worksheet, confirm that the correct sheet and required data are selected. Use Print Preview to inspect how the content will be divided across pages.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Fit and readability</h3>\n<p>Use orientation and scaling to make the content fit sensibly. Avoid reducing the worksheet so much that numbers, labels, or headings become difficult to read.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Page layout</h3>\n<p>Review margins and page breaks. If a worksheet is intended to be a report, give it a clear title and make sure column headings remain meaningful on printed pages.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Practical example</h3>\n<p>A teacher needs to print a class register with names and attendance for 40 learners. The teacher checks the print area, chooses an appropriate orientation, previews the page breaks, and confirms that names and attendance columns are readable.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Final check</h3>\n<p>Printing should be the final step after checking the data and layout. If the preview is wrong, adjust the worksheet or print settings before producing paper copies.</p>\n",
