      "notes": "\n<h3>1. Meaning of a slide layout</h3>\n<p>A slide layout determines the arrangement of placeholders for content such as a title, text, pictures, charts, or other objects.</p>\n<h3>2. Common layouts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Title slide:</b> introduces the presentation title and often the presenter.</li>\n<li><b>Title and Content:</b> provides a title and a main content area.</li>\n<li><b>Two Content:</b> provides two areas for comparing or grouping information.</li>\n<li><b>Blank:</b> provides a slide without predefined content placeholders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Choosing a layout</h3>\n<p>Choose the layout according to the type of information. A comparison may suit two content areas, while a title slide is appropriate for introducing the presentation.</p>\n<h3>4. Changing a layout</h3>\n<p>Changing a slide's layout can rearrange or provide placeholders. Check the slide afterwards because existing content may need repositioning.</p>\n<h3>5. Example</h3>\n<p>To compare advantages and disadvantages of solar energy, use a layout with two content areas and label each side clearly.</p>\n",
