      "notes": "\n<h3>1. Meaning of system maintenance records</h3>\n<p><strong>System maintenance records</strong> are documents that record work performed on a computer system to keep it operational, secure and reliable. They provide a history of repairs, updates, inspections, configuration changes and other maintenance activities.</p>\n<p>For example, when a school updates its student-management application to correct a marks-calculation defect, a maintenance record documents the problem, the correction and the tests performed afterward.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Why maintenance records are important</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Historical reference:</strong> Shows what changes have been made to the system.</li>\n<li><strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> Helps technicians investigate recurring problems by reviewing earlier incidents and repairs.</li>\n<li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Records who performed or authorised a maintenance activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Change tracking:</strong> Helps establish when and why a system component was changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Helps maintenance teams identify recurring faults and plan preventive work.</li>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> Records important security updates, configuration changes and corrective actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Planning:</strong> Helps organisations schedule future maintenance and allocate resources.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Information recorded in a maintenance record</h3>\n<p>A useful maintenance record commonly includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Record ID:</strong> A unique reference for the activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> When the maintenance occurred.</li>\n<li><strong>System or component:</strong> The computer, application or component affected.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem or reason:</strong> Why maintenance was required.</li>\n<li><strong>Work performed:</strong> The repair, update, inspection or change completed.</li>\n<li><strong>Person responsible:</strong> The technician or team that performed the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome:</strong> Whether the activity resolved the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Verification:</strong> Tests or checks performed after maintenance.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up action:</strong> Any unresolved issue or further work required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact fields depend on the organisation and the type of system being maintained.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Worked example: correcting a marks-calculation defect</h3>\n<p>A school's application incorrectly calculates a learner's average mark. A developer investigates and corrects the calculation.</p>\n\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example entry</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Record ID</td><td>MR024</td></tr>\n<tr><td>System</td><td>School marks application</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Problem</td><td>Average mark calculated incorrectly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Work performed</td><td>Corrected the average-calculation logic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Responsible person</td><td>Assigned software developer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Verification</td><td>Repeated the failed calculation test and checked relevant cases</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Original test passed; related test results recorded</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Follow-up</td><td>Monitor for recurrence and retain test evidence</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>This record allows another technician to understand the problem, see what was changed and determine what evidence supports the reported outcome.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Types of maintenance activities that may be recorded</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corrective maintenance:</strong> Work performed to fix faults discovered after the system begins operating.</li>\n<li><strong>Adaptive maintenance:</strong> Changes made to keep the system working when its environment changes, such as a new operating system or external service.</li>\n<li><strong>Perfective maintenance:</strong> Improvements made to performance, usability or functionality.</li>\n<li><strong>Preventive maintenance:</strong> Work intended to reduce the likelihood of future problems, such as applying appropriate security updates or inspecting equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, repairing a calculation defect is corrective maintenance, while improving a slow report-generation process is perfective maintenance.</p>\n\n<h3>6. How to maintain accurate records</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Record the problem or reason for the maintenance.</li>\n<li>Identify the affected system and component.</li>\n<li>Describe the work performed clearly and factually.</li>\n<li>Record the responsible person and date.</li>\n<li>Document the tests or checks used to verify the change.</li>\n<li>Record the outcome, including any remaining problems.</li>\n<li>Store the record securely where authorised staff can retrieve it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Records should be created promptly and updated when further work is completed. Important changes should be traceable to supporting evidence, such as test results or approved change requests.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Maintenance records versus other documentation</h3>\n<p><strong>Technical documentation</strong> explains how a system is structured, configured and operated.</p>\n<p><strong>Program documentation</strong> explains a program's code, logic and implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>System maintenance records</strong> document the maintenance work actually performed and its outcome.</p>\n<p>For example, a document describing the marks database structure is technical documentation. An explanation of the average-calculation function is program documentation. A dated entry showing that the function was corrected and retested is a maintenance record.</p>\n\n<h3>8. Qualities of good maintenance records</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accurate:</strong> Describes the work and outcome truthfully.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete:</strong> Includes the details needed to understand the activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Timely:</strong> Is recorded soon after the work is performed.</li>\n<li><strong>Traceable:</strong> Identifies the affected system, date and responsible person.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear:</strong> Avoids vague descriptions such as “computer fixed”.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure:</strong> Is protected from unauthorised access or alteration.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrievable:</strong> Can be found when investigating problems or planning maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>9. Common mistakes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Recording only that maintenance occurred without describing the work.</li>\n<li>Failing to record the result of post-maintenance testing.</li>\n<li>Leaving out the affected component or date.</li>\n<li>Claiming a problem was resolved when verification was incomplete.</li>\n<li>Failing to record unresolved issues and follow-up actions.</li>\n<li>Allowing records to be lost, altered without trace or accessed by unauthorised people.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>10. Key takeaway</h3>\n<p>System maintenance records provide an accurate history of repairs, updates and other maintenance work. They help organisations troubleshoot problems, verify changes, maintain accountability and plan future work.</p>\n",
