      "notes": "\n<h2>Cloud collaboration and file sharing</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Meaning of cloud collaboration</h3>\n<p>Cloud collaboration is the process of working together on documents, files, or projects using internet-based services that allow authorised users to access shared resources.</p>\n<p>Instead of sending a new copy of a document to every group member, users can work on a shared online file. Depending on the service, they may edit it simultaneously, leave comments, and see changes made by others.</p>\n\n<h3>2. How cloud file sharing works</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>A user uploads or creates a file in a cloud service.</li>\n<li>The user chooses specific people or creates a sharing link.</li>\n<li>The user assigns permissions that determine what recipients can do.</li>\n<li>Recipients access the file through an authorised account or link.</li>\n<li>Changes may be saved and made available to other authorised users.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. File-sharing permissions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Viewer:</strong> Can view the file but normally cannot change its contents.</li>\n<li><strong>Commenter:</strong> Can view the file and add comments or suggestions where supported.</li>\n<li><strong>Editor:</strong> Can change the file's contents and may be able to share it further, depending on the service settings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Permissions differ between services. The owner should check the actual options before sharing sensitive work.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Sharing with named people and sharing links</h3>\n<p><strong>Named-person sharing</strong> grants access to selected accounts. It helps limit access to intended recipients.</p>\n<p><strong>Link sharing</strong> allows people who meet the link's access requirements to open a file. A link restricted to specified accounts is safer for private work than a link that anyone can open.</p>\n<p>A link can be forwarded to other people. Therefore, having a link does not always mean the recipient is the only person who can access the file.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Working together on shared files</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Several users may edit the same document at the same time if the service supports live collaboration.</li>\n<li>Comments help group members discuss particular parts of a document.</li>\n<li>Version history may allow users to inspect earlier changes or restore a previous version.</li>\n<li>Users should coordinate edits when a service does not handle simultaneous changes well.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>6. Safe collaboration practices</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Give each person only the permissions needed for their task.</li>\n<li>Check the recipients and access settings before sharing.</li>\n<li>Avoid public links for confidential documents.</li>\n<li>Do not place passwords or private personal information in files shared with unauthorised people.</li>\n<li>Remove access when a person no longer needs it.</li>\n<li>Use version history carefully to recover from unwanted changes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>7. Advantages and risks</h3>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> easier teamwork, access from different locations, fewer duplicate copies, and faster sharing of updates.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks:</strong> accidental disclosure, unwanted editing, forwarded links, conflicting changes, and dependence on internet access for many online features.</p>\n\n<h3>Remember</h3>\n<p>Cloud collaboration enables people to work on shared online resources. Safe collaboration depends on choosing the right recipients, controlling permissions, and reviewing changes.</p>\n",
