      "notes": "\n<h2>Types and approaches of artificial intelligence</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Rule-based artificial intelligence</h3>\n<p>Rule-based AI uses explicitly defined rules and conditions to produce results. The system follows instructions created by people rather than learning those rules automatically from examples.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> A troubleshooting system may recommend checking a network cable when a connection is unavailable and a particular condition is met.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> It may struggle with situations that its rules do not cover.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Machine learning</h3>\n<p>Machine learning (ML) is an approach in which computer systems learn patterns from data to make predictions, classify information, or support decisions.</p>\n<p>Instead of manually writing every rule for every possible input, developers train a model using suitable data and evaluate how well it performs.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> An email filter can learn patterns associated with spam messages from labelled examples.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Generative artificial intelligence</h3>\n<p>Generative AI produces new content, such as text, images, audio, video, or computer code, based on a prompt or other input.</p>\n<p>Many generative AI systems use machine-learning models trained on large collections of data.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> A language model can draft a paragraph explaining a scientific concept.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> Generated content may contain errors, invented details, or unsuitable material.</p>\n\n<h3>4. How the approaches relate</h3>\n<p>These categories describe different aspects of AI and are not always mutually exclusive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule-based AI follows explicitly defined rules.</li>\n<li>Machine learning uses patterns learned from data.</li>\n<li>Generative AI focuses on producing new content and often uses machine learning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An AI application may combine rules and machine-learning models. Generative AI is commonly built using machine learning, so these categories should not be treated as three completely separate technologies.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Choosing the right approach</h3>\n<p>The appropriate approach depends on the task.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clear, fixed conditions:</strong> A rule-based system may be suitable when the rules are known and manageable.</li>\n<li><strong>Finding patterns in examples:</strong> Machine learning may be useful when reliable data is available.</li>\n<li><strong>Creating drafts or other content:</strong> Generative AI may be suitable when outputs can be reviewed and corrected.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>6. Important considerations</h3>\n<p>AI developers and users should consider data quality, accuracy, privacy, fairness, cost, and the consequences of errors. A system should be tested against the task it is intended to perform.</p>\n\n<h3>Remember</h3>\n<p>Rule-based AI follows defined rules, machine learning learns patterns from data, and generative AI creates content. A single system may combine more than one approach.</p>\n",
